Getting *your own* data into a `.tsra`. Every command below is run against the real binary on every
build, so nothing here can drift from the tool. Digests and generated names are globbed with `[..]`; the
structural lines are matched exactly.

# === 1. The honest fallback: preserve any file bit-faithfully as a `blob`. ===
# === Reach for this when nothing understands the format yet — you still get identity, integrity, ===
# === and provenance, and you can decode it later without having lost anything in the meantime.   ===
# === `--meta study=…` satisfies the blob schema's RECOMMENDED field; omit it and you get a WARN.  ===

```
$ tessera ingest blob notes.txt console.tsra --name console-export --timestamp 2024-01-01T00:00:00Z --meta study=DEMO-2024-01
ingested [..] (blob) -> console.tsra

```

# === Always verify before trusting a byte — the seal plus every block digest. ===

```
$ tessera verify console.tsra
OK  console.tsra verified (1 blocks)

```

# === The bytes come back verbatim, so preservation is a promise you can check, not a claim. ===
# === `data` is the block name a blob product uses; `tessera ls <file>` lists them.            ===

```
$ tessera extract console.tsra data roundtrip.txt
extracted data (117 bytes) -> roundtrip.txt

```

# === 2. A declarative spec — one file that produces a whole sealed collection, reproducibly. ===
# === Re-running it on the same inputs yields byte-identical products; this is the smallest    ===
# === spec that does something real, and `--spec` scales from here to a full study.            ===

```
$ tessera ingest --spec minimal-spec.toml --out ./study
ingested collection blake3:2e49fbe123f6acd4e257b338c60c4763d383f83c4eb91282d3c3ef9e45a5c4c0 (1 members) -> ./study
  - blake3:ae0d02568b647a1725f2fc7cb024265581a4f3c5d62c87f936a90a110140ae1a -> blake3_ae0d02568b647a1725f2fc7cb024265581a4f3c5d62c87f936a90a110140ae1a.tsra

```

# === Members are written under their CONTENT id, not their human name — identity, not filename. ===
# === `collection ls` is how you get from a name to a file.                                      ===

```
$ tessera collection ls ./study/collection.json
raw     blob       blake3:ae0d02568b647a1725f2fc7cb024265581a4f3c5d62c87f936a90a110140ae1a console-export           [blake3:9d45728fbedc…]

```

# === Verify the collection as a unit: its own seal, then every member present, intact and pinned. ===

```
$ tessera collection verify ./study/collection.json
OK  ./study/collection.json verified (1 members, recursive)

```
